{"Hi, I'm [your name].":[1,0.35,1.355],"Our organisation works to...":[1,2.055,1.881],"We focus on youth, women and vulnerable communities.":[1,4.285,3.518],"I work as a project coordinator. (habit / always)":[1,8.153,3.622],"Currently, I'm preparing a report. (now)":[1,12.125,3.03],"My name is Sara. I work as a project officer at a community organisation in Minya. Every day, I visit villages, meet women's groups and listen to their needs. I love my job because I can see the change.":[1,15.506,12.759],"Hi, I'm [your name]. I work as a programme coordinator at a local development organisation in Egypt. Our organisation works to create opportunities for young people and women in vulnerable communities. We support them through training, small grants and partnerships with local NGOs. Currently, we're implementing a project on youth employment in two governorates. My role covers planning, follow-up with partners and reporting, but what drives me is seeing real change in people's lives.":[1,28.615,28.673],"I work as a programme coordinator at a local development organisation in Egypt.":[1,57.638,4.923],"We operate in five governorates across Egypt.":[1,62.911,2.833],"Currently, we're implementing a project on...":[1,66.093,2.961],"Our organisation was founded in 2010. We work in education and health. We support children who left school and help them return. We also train mothers on child nutrition.":[1,69.404,11.169],"What does your organisation do, and who does it help?":[1,80.923,2.984],"Our organisation works to create opportunities for young people and women in vulnerable communities.":[1,84.256,6.293],"My role covers...":[1,90.899,1.173],"What drives our work is...":[1,92.422,1.8],"In the development sector, people often ask: What does your organisation do? A good answer is short and clear. Say who you help, what you do, and why it matters.":[1,94.571,10.519],"Describe a normal working day for you.":[1,105.44,2.485],"We support them through training, small grants and partnerships with local NGOs.":[1,108.274,5.155],"I've been working in development for five years.":[1,113.779,2.728],"I'm in charge of...":[1,116.857,1.231],"Karim is a monitoring officer. He collects data from the field every week. He checks if activities happen on time. Then he writes a short report for the project manager.":[1,118.438,9.741],"What do you like most about working in development?":[1,128.529,2.868],"Currently, we're implementing a project on youth employment in two governorates.":[1,131.746,4.586],"Our main donors are...":[1,136.682,1.683],"We have a team of twenty staff and volunteers.":[1,138.716,2.775],"Many local NGOs in Egypt work with international partners. These partners give funding and technical support. In return, local NGOs bring their knowledge of the community.":[1,141.84,11.111],"Introduce a colleague: their name, their role and what they do.":[1,153.301,3.808],"My role covers planning, follow-up with partners and reporting, but what drives me is seeing real change in people's lives.":[1,157.459,7.477],"I'm passionate about...":[1,165.286,1.254],"Nice to meet you. What do you do?":[1,166.89,2.287],"At a networking event, introduce yourself in thirty seconds. Say your name, your role, and one interesting fact about your work. Then ask the other person a question.":[1,169.527,9.985],"You meet a donor at a conference. Introduce yourself in 30 seconds.":[1,179.862,4.145],"Hello, my name is Omar. I work for a youth organisation in Alexandria. We help young people find jobs through training and coaching. Currently, we are working with twenty local companies.":[1,184.356,11.401],"The project aims to improve employment opportunities for young people in Upper Egypt.":[1,196.107,4.923],"The project aims to...":[1,201.38,1.683],"The main problem we're addressing is...":[1,203.413,2.159],"We train teachers in order to improve education quality.":[1,205.923,3.448],"Many children left school due to poverty.":[1,209.721,2.554],"The project aims to reduce school dropout in Upper Egypt. It targets 3,000 children aged 8 to 14. Activities include remedial classes, school meals and awareness sessions for parents.":[1,212.625,12.307],"The project aims to improve employment opportunities for young people in Upper Egypt. The main problem we're addressing is the gap between what young people learn and what the local market needs. Our target group is 1,500 young women and men aged 18 to 29. Key activities include vocational training, coaching and internships with local businesses. In order to make this sustainable, we work with local NGOs so that they can continue the services after the project ends.":[1,225.282,28.723],"The main problem we're addressing is the gap between what young people learn and what the local market needs.":[1,254.355,5.933],"Our target group is...":[1,260.637,1.556],"Key activities include...":[1,262.543,1.672],"A good project starts with a clear problem. For example, many young women cannot find jobs because they lack skills and experience. The project then explains how it will solve this problem.":[1,264.565,11.981],"What problem does your project solve?":[2,0.35,2.345],"Our target group is 1,500 young women and men aged 18 to 29.":[2,3.045,5.341],"In order to achieve this, we...":[2,8.736,1.939],"By the end of the project, we expect...":[2,11.025,2.31],"Our water project works in ten villages. We repair water pumps, train local committees and teach families about hygiene. As a result, children get sick less often.":[2,13.685,10.31],"Who is your target group, and why did you choose them?":[2,24.345,3.251],"Key activities include vocational training, coaching and internships with local businesses.":[2,27.945,5.468],"The project runs for two years.":[2,33.764,2.101],"It targets women in rural areas.":[2,36.215,2.334],"Every project has inputs, activities, outputs and outcomes. Inputs are money and people. Activities are what we do. Outputs are what we produce. Outcomes are the changes in people's lives.":[2,38.899,13.317],"Describe three main activities of your project.":[2,52.565,2.914],"In order to make this sustainable, we work with local NGOs so that they can continue the services after the project ends.":[2,55.829,7.303],"We work in partnership with...":[2,63.482,1.637],"The expected outcome is...":[2,65.469,1.625],"The project is funded by the European Union and implemented by three local partners. Each partner works in one governorate, so that we can reach more people.":[2,67.444,9.381],"What change do you expect by the end of the project?":[2,77.175,2.868],"This will lead to...":[2,80.393,1.196],"The project is funded by...":[2,81.939,2.02],"In order to make the project sustainable, we train local volunteers. After the project ends, they can continue the activities with support from the community.":[2,84.309,9.752],"How will the project continue after the funding ends?":[2,94.411,3.077],"Our project helps small farmers in Fayoum increase their income. We train them on modern irrigation and connect them to markets. By the end of the project, we expect 500 farmers to earn twenty percent more.":[2,97.838,12.585],"Shall we get started?":[2,110.773,1.265],"Could I just come in here?":[2,112.388,1.533],"We should share the report before Thursday.":[2,114.271,2.334],"Could you send me the attendance list?":[2,116.954,2.136],"Before the meeting, the coordinator sends the agenda to all partners. The agenda has three points: activities, budget and challenges. Each partner prepares a short update.":[2,119.441,10.6],"Shall we get started? We have three items on the agenda: the work plan for next quarter, the division of governorates, and the community outreach strategy. Let's start with the work plan. Mona, could you give us a quick update? ... I see your point, but I'm concerned that working in all governorates at once might affect quality. Could we start with two and expand later? How can we avoid duplication with other partners in the same area?":[2,130.391,25.147],"We have three items on the agenda: the work plan for next quarter, the division of governorates, and the community outreach strategy.":[2,155.888,8.022],"I see your point, but...":[2,164.26,1.695],"Let's park that for now and come back to it.":[2,166.305,2.74],"In meetings, it is normal to disagree. The important thing is to stay polite. You can say: I understand your point, but I see it differently.":[2,169.395,8.777],"Open a meeting: welcome people and present the agenda.":[2,178.522,3.738],"Let's start with the work plan.":[2,182.611,1.858],"How can we avoid duplication between partners?":[2,184.818,3.042],"Who's going to take this forward?":[2,188.21,1.834],"Coordination between partners helps avoid duplication. If two organisations work in the same village, they should share information and divide the activities.":[2,190.394,9.729],"Politely disagree with this idea: 'We should cancel the training.'":[2,200.474,3.878],"Mona, could you give us a quick update?":[2,204.701,2.218],"Let's go around the table.":[2,207.269,1.672],"Sorry, could you repeat that?":[2,209.291,1.846],"At the end of a meeting, someone should summarise the decisions. Who will do what, and by when? These are called action points.":[2,211.486,8.046],"Ask someone to explain a point you didn't understand.":[2,219.882,3.495],"... I see your point, but I'm concerned that working in all governorates at once might affect quality.":[2,223.727,5.735],"What do you mean by...?":[2,229.812,1.277],"I'd like to add something.":[3,0.35,1.509],"Online meetings are common now. Mute your microphone when you are not speaking, and use the chat to share links. Speak slowly and clearly.":[3,2.209,8.197],"Summarise the action points of a meeting you attended.":[3,10.756,2.926],"Could we start with two and expand later?":[3,14.032,2.577],"Let's summarise the action points.":[3,16.959,2.101],"Let's meet again next week.":[3,19.41,1.683],"If you don't understand something in a meeting, ask. Most people prefer a question to a mistake. You could say: Sorry, could you explain that again?":[3,21.444,9.079],"Close a meeting and agree on the next date.":[3,30.873,2.31],"Good morning, everyone. Let's start. First, each partner will give a two-minute update. Then we will discuss the training schedule. Finally, we will agree on the next steps. Mona, would you like to start?":[3,33.533,12.376],"By the end of the project, we reached 2,400 beneficiaries, sixty percent of them women.":[3,46.259,5.863],"We reached 2,400 beneficiaries, 60% of them women.":[3,52.472,4.493],"Compared to the baseline, ... increased by...":[3,57.315,2.728],"The number of trained teachers increased from 40 to 75.":[3,60.394,3.773],"Results this year were better than last year.":[3,64.517,2.705],"Monitoring means checking progress regularly. Evaluation means looking at the results at a specific time, for example in the middle or at the end of the project.":[3,67.572,9.195],"By the end of the project, we reached 2,400 beneficiaries, sixty percent of them women. Compared to the baseline, the employment rate among participants increased from twelve to thirty-five percent. However, the indicator on women's leadership was lower than expected, mainly due to mobility challenges. The evaluation found that mentoring was the most effective activity. One key lesson learned is that we need to involve families from the start.":[3,77.117,27.307],"Compared to the baseline, the employment rate among participants increased from twelve to thirty-five percent.":[3,104.774,5.875],"The indicator shows that...":[3,110.998,1.741],"One key lesson learned is...":[3,113.09,1.765],"An indicator is something we can measure. For example, the number of women who started a business, or the percentage of children who passed their exams.":[3,115.205,8.44],"What is the difference between monitoring and evaluation?":[3,123.995,3.17],"However, the indicator on women's leadership was lower than expected, mainly due to mobility challenges.":[3,127.514,6.769],"The evaluation found that...":[3,134.633,1.823],"To ensure sustainability, we...":[3,136.806,2.241],"Before the project, we did a baseline survey. Only 20% of young people had a job. After two years, the number increased to 45%. This is a big improvement.":[3,139.397,10.89],"Describe one indicator from your project and its result.":[3,150.637,3.309],"The evaluation found that mentoring was the most effective activity.":[3,154.295,4.052],"We collect data every month.":[3,158.697,1.672],"The target was 500, and we reached 620.":[3,160.719,3.402],"Data can be quantitative, like numbers and percentages, or qualitative, like stories and opinions. Good reports use both.":[3,164.471,7.883],"Compare the situation before and after your project.":[3,172.704,3.355],"One key lesson learned is that we need to involve families from the start.":[3,176.409,4.017],"This is lower than expected.":[3,180.776,1.695],"According to the survey...":[3,182.821,1.498],"The evaluation found that the training was useful, but it was too short. Participants asked for more practical sessions. This is an important lesson for the next project.":[3,184.669,9.845],"Talk about one lesson you learned from a project.":[3,194.864,2.717],"We used focus group discussions.":[3,197.931,2.38],"The data shows a clear improvement.":[3,200.661,2.194],"Beneficiary feedback is very important. We use complaint boxes, phone lines and community meetings to hear what people think about our work.":[3,203.205,8.731],"How do you collect feedback from beneficiaries?":[3,212.286,2.647],"Our project reached 2,400 people, more than the target of 2,000. The number of girls in school increased by fifteen percent compared to the baseline. However, attendance was lower in winter because of bad roads.":[3,215.283,12.98],"Good morning, everyone.":[3,228.613,1.451],"Today I'd like to share our experience in...":[3,230.414,3.007],"Let me start with a short story.":[3,233.771,2.032],"The project was launched in 2024.":[4,0.35,2.752],"More than 500 women have been trained.":[4,3.452,2.519],"A good presentation has three parts: an opening, a body and a closing. In the opening, say who you are and what you will talk about.":[4,6.321,7.883],"Good morning, everyone. Today I'd like to share our experience in supporting women-led businesses in rural Egypt. Let me start with a short story. Two years ago, Amal was selling bread from her home. The programme was launched in 2024 and has been implemented in three governorates. Today, Amal employs four women from her village. The key takeaway here is that small, flexible grants can create real change. To sum up... I'm happy to take any questions.":[4,14.554,28.294],"Today I'd like to share our experience in supporting women-led businesses in rural Egypt.":[4,43.198,5.248],"The programme was launched in 2024 and has been implemented in...":[4,48.795,4.238],"The key takeaway here is...":[4,53.383,1.637],"Stories help people remember. Instead of only showing numbers, tell the story of one person who benefited from your project.":[4,55.37,7.419],"Open a presentation: greet, introduce yourself and give the plan.":[4,63.139,4.714],"To sum up...":[4,68.202,0.813],"I'm happy to take any questions.":[4,69.365,1.962],"Signposting words help the audience follow you: first, next, then, finally. They show where you are in the presentation.":[4,71.677,7.86],"Tell the story of one person who benefited from a project.":[4,79.887,3.448],"Two years ago, Amal was selling bread from her home.":[4,83.685,3.425],"Thank you for having me.":[4,87.46,1.277],"I'll be brief.":[4,89.087,1.265],"The training centre was opened in 2023. Since then, more than 800 young people have been trained. Most of them were hired by local companies.":[4,90.702,9.543],"Present three numbers from your work and explain them.":[4,100.596,3.03],"The programme was launched in 2024 and has been implemented in three governorates.":[4,103.976,5.248],"Let me show you some numbers.":[4,109.574,1.8],"This brings me to my next point.":[4,111.723,1.997],"When you answer questions, listen to the whole question first. If you don't know the answer, it's fine to say: That's a good question. I'll check and get back to you.":[4,114.07,10.043],"Answer this question: 'What was your biggest challenge?'":[4,124.463,3.262],"Today, Amal employs four women from her village.":[4,128.075,3.309],"In conclusion...":[4,131.734,1.138],"Thank you for your attention.":[4,133.222,1.707],"Practise your presentation out loud at least three times. Time yourself. Most speakers talk too fast when they are nervous, so slow down.":[4,135.278,9.079],"Close your presentation with a strong final message.":[4,144.707,2.868],"Good afternoon. My name is Heba, and today I'll talk about our women's savings groups in Sohag. First, I'll explain the idea. Then, I'll share the results. Finally, I'll tell you about Fatma, one of our members.":[4,147.925,13.886],"Thank you for your time.":[4,162.16,1.358],"We'd like to propose a project that...":[4,163.869,2.241],"This aligns with your priorities on...":[4,166.46,2.31],"If we get the grant, we will hire two trainers.":[4,169.12,2.728],"If we had a bigger budget, we would work in more villages.":[4,172.198,3.599],"Donors publish calls for proposals. Each call explains the priorities, the budget, the eligible countries and the deadline. Read it carefully before you start writing.":[4,176.147,10.403],"Thank you for your time. We'd like to propose a project that helps local organisations in Egypt access funding and manage it well. This aligns with your priorities on localisation and civil society. If we receive funding, we'll be able to train forty organisations and give small grants to twenty of them. Let me be transparent about the challenges: registration procedures can take time. If needed, would you be open to a three-month inception phase?":[4,186.9,27.4],"We'd like to propose a project that helps local organisations in Egypt access funding and manage it well.":[4,214.649,6.734],"If we receive funding, we'll be able to...":[4,221.733,2.682],"Would you be open to...?":[4,224.765,1.231],"A concept note is a short version of the proposal, usually two to five pages. It explains the problem, the solution, the target group and the budget.":[4,226.346,9.404],"Pitch a project idea in one minute.":[4,236.1,2.38],"This aligns with your priorities on localisation and civil society.":[4,238.83,4.563],"Let me be transparent about the challenges.":[4,243.742,2.577],"Can we find a middle ground?":[4,246.67,1.509],"Donors want to see that your project matches their priorities. Use their words in your proposal, and explain clearly how your work supports their goals.":[5,0.35,9.23],"Explain why your organisation is the right partner for this project.":[5,9.93,3.75],"If we receive funding, we'll be able to train forty organisations and give small grants to twenty of them.":[5,14.03,6.362],"The call for proposals closes on...":[5,20.742,2.299],"Our organisation has experience in...":[5,23.391,2.601],"If we receive funding, we will open three new learning centres. If we had more time, we would also train teachers in neighbouring villages.":[5,26.341,8.348],"Ask a donor a question about the call for proposals.":[5,35.039,2.926],"Let me be transparent about the challenges: registration procedures can take time.":[5,38.315,5.027],"We have a strong track record.":[5,43.692,1.8],"The total requested amount is...":[5,45.841,2.055],"Negotiation is not a fight. Both sides want the project to succeed. Be honest about challenges and come with possible solutions.":[5,48.246,7.651],"Ask for a three-month extension and explain why.":[5,56.247,3.111],"If needed, would you be open to a three-month inception phase?":[5,59.709,3.553],"Could you clarify the eligibility criteria?":[5,63.611,3.135],"We are flexible on the timeline.":[5,67.096,1.858],"After you submit a proposal, the donor may ask questions. Answer quickly and clearly. A good relationship with the donor is as important as a good proposal.":[5,69.304,9.16],"Respond politely when a donor says no.":[5,78.814,2.554],"Thank you for meeting us. Our organisation has ten years of experience in youth employment. We'd like to propose a project in Assiut that trains 600 young people. If we receive funding, we will start in March.":[5,81.718,12.283],"At the time, I was coordinating a project with twelve local partners.":[5,94.351,4.342],"At the time, I was leading...":[5,99.043,1.95],"The challenge was that...":[5,101.344,1.474],"I was working in Minya when the project started.":[5,103.168,2.67],"While we were preparing the training, the donor called.":[5,106.189,2.926],"Many NGO interviews use behavioural questions. For example: Tell me about a time when you solved a problem. The interviewer wants a real story, not a general answer.":[5,109.464,10.937],"At the time, I was coordinating a project with twelve local partners. The challenge was that most partners had never managed an international grant before. What I did was design a simple coaching programme and visit each partner every month. As a result, all twelve partners submitted their reports on time, and three of them later received direct funding. What I learned from this is that capacity building needs patience and follow-up.":[5,120.751,25.612],"The challenge was that most partners had never managed an international grant before.":[5,146.712,4.76],"What I did was...":[5,151.823,1.324],"As a result, ...":[5,153.496,0.975],"The STAR method helps you answer: Situation, Task, Action, Result. Start with the context, explain your role, describe what you did, and finish with the result.":[5,154.821,11.006],"Tell me about yourself in one minute.":[5,166.178,2.183],"What I did was design a simple coaching programme and visit each partner every month.":[5,168.71,4.795],"What I learned from this is...":[5,173.855,1.741],"That's a great question — let me think for a second.":[5,175.947,3.135],"When I was working in Qena, our training was cancelled because of bad weather. I quickly moved it online and called every participant. In the end, ninety percent attended.":[5,179.431,10.449],"Why do you want to work in development?":[5,190.23,1.927],"As a result, all twelve partners submitted their reports on time, and three of them later received direct funding.":[5,192.507,6.757],"I have five years of experience in...":[5,199.614,2.519],"One of my strengths is...":[5,202.484,1.567],"Prepare for the interview. Read about the organisation, its projects and its values. Think about how your experience matches the job description.":[5,204.401,8.777],"Tell me about a time you solved a problem. Use STAR.":[5,213.528,3.379],"What I learned from this is that capacity building needs patience and follow-up.":[5,217.257,4.505],"I'm working on improving my...":[5,222.111,1.892],"I'm interested in this role because...":[5,224.354,2.357],"It's normal to pause before answering. A short silence looks more confident than 'umm'. Take a breath, think, then answer.":[5,227.06,7.442],"What is your biggest strength, and one thing you are improving?":[6,0.35,3.39],"Could you tell me more about the team?":[6,4.09,1.811],"Thank you for the opportunity.":[6,6.251,1.927],"At the end of the interview, ask one or two questions. For example: What does success look like in this role? This shows interest and preparation.":[6,8.528,9.23],"Ask the interviewer two good questions.":[6,18.108,2.752],"At the time, I was leading a team of five facilitators. The challenge was that many women couldn't attend morning sessions. What I did was move the sessions to the afternoon and provide childcare. As a result, attendance doubled.":[6,21.21,13.398],"In my view, the biggest challenge in our sector today is localisation.":[6,34.958,4.481],"In my view, the biggest challenge is...":[6,39.789,2.589],"Localisation means...":[6,42.728,1.591],"In my view, local NGOs should get more direct funding.":[6,44.669,4.203],"I partly agree, but I think the problem is bigger.":[6,49.222,3.332],"Localisation means shifting power and funding to local organisations. Many people believe local groups understand their communities better and can respond faster.":[6,52.904,9.381],"In my view, the biggest challenge in our sector today is localisation. Localisation means giving local organisations more power, more direct funding and more decision-making. There's a growing need for this, especially with aid cuts. However, I partly agree with people who say local organisations need stronger systems first. Moving forward, we should prioritise long-term partnerships, not short projects.":[6,62.634,25.31],"Localisation means giving local organisations more power, more direct funding and more decision-making.":[6,88.294,6.281],"There's a growing need for...":[6,94.925,1.393],"I partly agree, but...":[6,96.668,1.672],"Climate change affects Egypt in many ways: rising temperatures, water shortages and pressure on agriculture. Development projects now include climate adaptation.":[6,98.69,10.786],"What is the biggest development challenge in your governorate?":[6,109.826,2.995],"There's a growing need for this, especially with aid cuts.":[6,113.171,3.309],"Looking back, I've noticed that...":[6,116.83,2.206],"Moving forward, we should prioritise...":[6,119.386,2.612],"Youth unemployment is a big challenge in the region. Training alone is not enough. Young people also need networks, experience and access to finance.":[6,122.348,9.207],"Give your opinion on localisation in two minutes.":[6,131.905,2.926],"However, I partly agree with people who say local organisations need stronger systems first.":[6,135.18,5.584],"From my experience...":[6,141.115,1.428],"That's a fair point.":[6,142.893,1.196],"Women's economic empowerment is more than giving loans. It also means changing social norms, sharing care work and supporting women in decision-making.":[6,144.439,9.033],"Agree and disagree with: 'Training alone solves unemployment.'":[6,153.821,3.785],"Moving forward, we should prioritise long-term partnerships, not short projects.":[6,157.956,5.48],"On the other hand...":[6,163.786,1.068],"I'm not sure I agree.":[6,165.204,1.672],"Aid budgets are decreasing in many countries. Organisations need to diversify their funding, build partnerships with the private sector and show clear results.":[6,167.226,9.091],"How does climate change affect the people you work with?":[6,176.666,3.019],"The evidence shows that...":[6,180.035,1.579],"In the long term...":[6,181.964,1.126],"Digital tools are changing development work. Mobile phones help us collect data, send money and reach people in remote areas. But not everyone has access.":[6,183.44,9.474],"Compare your English today with day one. What changed?":[6,193.264,3.448],"In my view, the biggest challenge for local NGOs is sustainable funding. On the other hand, they have strong community trust. In the long term, they need better systems and long partnerships, not short projects.":[6,197.062,13.758],"accountability":[6,211.17,1.103],"action plan":[6,212.623,1.126],"activity":[6,214.099,0.894],"advocacy":[6,215.343,0.836],"agenda":[6,216.529,0.72],"aid":[6,217.598,0.697],"allocation":[7,0.35,0.94],"annex":[7,1.64,0.813],"annual report":[7,2.803,1.173],"applicant":[7,4.326,0.685],"assessment":[7,5.361,0.743],"assumption":[7,6.454,0.813],"attendance sheet":[7,7.616,1.068],"audit":[7,9.034,0.569],"awareness session":[7,9.953,1.103],"baseline":[7,11.406,0.871],"beneficiary":[7,12.627,1.033],"best practice":[7,14.01,1.103],"board of directors":[7,15.463,1.231],"budget":[7,17.044,0.731],"budget line":[7,18.125,1.08],"budget revision":[7,19.555,1.242],"burn rate":[7,21.147,0.894],"call for proposals":[7,22.391,1.3],"capacity building":[7,24.041,1.3],"case study":[7,25.692,1.022],"cash transfer":[7,27.063,1.289],"child protection":[7,28.702,1.219],"civil society":[7,30.271,1.196],"climate adaptation":[7,31.817,1.556],"co-financing":[7,33.722,1.219],"code